  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 951 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nAn upper level shortwave trough looks to sweep over the \nMississippi River Valley and towards the Northeastern U.S. on \nSaturday. By Sunday and through early next week, upper ridging is \nshown to build over the southern Plains and Southeastern U.S. \nMeanwhile, surface high pressure is expected to remain parked over\nthe Appalachians for much of this time. This feature does shift \nsouth over the northern Gulf coast by Tuesday, however. Overall, \nanticipating daily chances of showers and storms, mainly during \nthe afternoon and evening hours. Expect higher chances of showers \nand storms on Saturday due to the aforementioned upper shortwave \ntrough swinging over the region, with chances decreasing for \nSunday through Tuesday. PWATs will remain elevated, but bulk shear\nwill likely be 20 knots or less throughout the period. Therefore,\nsevere weather is not anticipated, but frequent lightning and \nheavy rain will be the main threats with any storms.\n\nAs for temperatures, highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s with \nlows in the lower to mid 70s will be common. With elevated \nmoisture persisting, afternoon heat index values are forecast to \nmostly range between 98-104 degrees through the weekend, then \nincrease a bit by early next week as rain chances decrease. This \nresults in a Moderate HeatRisk for north Alabama and Southern \nMiddle Tennessee this weekend, with some locations in the Major \ncategory by Monday. This will be something to watch in case Heat \nAdvisories become necessary for all or portions of the local area.\nRegardless, please remember heat safety! Drink plenty of water, \ntake frequent breaks in the shade, and never leave people or pets \nin vehicles!",
